// A cyclic `once` the compile-time check cannot see, which the lineage
// cons-list exists for: forcing `seed` calls `apply`, which calls the function
// value it was handed, `read_seed`, which reads `seed` again. A call through a
// function value adds nothing to the check's graph (analysis.CheckOnceCycles),
// so the run time reports the cycle.
//
// The re-entry is visible only because the frame the force created is the
// frame `read_seed` received, so the answer is the raw diagnostic on stderr
// and exit 1, with "start" already on stdout.
//
// A `sync.Once`-shaped lowering with no cycle test would DEADLOCK here rather
// than report, which is why rt.OnceCell tests the lineage before taking the
// lock.
import std::io

fn read_seed() -> Int
    seed
end

fn apply(f: () -> Int) -> Int
    f()
end

once seed: Int = apply(read_seed) + 1

fn main()
    io.print("start")
    io.print("seed ${seed}")
end
